readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>Just where the breath of life his nostrils drew,
A charge of snuff the ''wily'' virgin threw.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., v. 82.}}}}
='''Syn.''' ''Cunning'', ''Artful'', ''Sly'', etc. (see ''cunning''<sup>1</sup>), designing, deceitful, foxy, diplomatic, delusive, insidious.

'''wily-beguile†''', ''n.'' The deceiving of one’s self in attempting to deceive another: used only in the phrase ''to play wily-beguile'' (or ''wily-beguily'').

{{smaller block|They, ''playing wily-beguile'' themselves, think it enough inwardly to favour the truth, though outwardly they curry favour.
{{right|''J. Bradford'', Writings (Parker Soc., 1848), I. 375.}}}}

{{smaller block|“''Playing wily-beguile''”: deceiving. A proverbial expression. Vide ''Ray'', Proverbs (ed. 1817), p. 46.
{{right|(Note to the above passage.)}}}}

{{smaller block|<poem>''Ch.'' I am fully resolved.
''P.'' Well, yet Cherea looke to it, that you ''play'' not now ''wily beguily'' your selfe.</poem>
{{right|''Terence in English'' (1614). (''Nares.'')}}}}

