readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') To gain by competition or conquest; take, as from an opponent or enemy; obtain as victor.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The Emperour Alexaunder Aunterid to come;
He ''wan'' all the world & at his wille aght.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 315.}}
Those proud titles thou hast ''won of'' me.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., v. 4. 79.}}
King Richard ''wan'' another strong hold, . . . from whence y<sup>e</sup> Monks being expulsed, he reposed there all his store.
{{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 22.}}
It had been an ancient maxim of the Greeks that no more acceptable gifts can be offered in the temples of the gods than the trophies ''won'' from an enemy in battle.
{{right|''Lecky'', Europ. Morals, II. 262.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') To earn: as, to ''win'' one’s bread.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He syneweth nat that so ''wynneth'' his fode.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), xxiii. 15.}}}}
'''2.''' To obtain; derive; get: as, to ''win'' ore from a mine.
{{smaller block|<poem>But alle thing hath tyme;
The day is short, and it is passed pryme;
And yet ne ''wan'' I nothing in this day.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Friar’s Tale, l. 179.}}
In these two places the prisoners are engaged in quarrying and cutting stone: at Borghamn, they ''win'' stone on account of the Government; at Tjurkö, granite for private contractors.
{{right|''Ribton-Turner'', Vagrants and Vagrancy, p. 508.}}}}
'''3.''' To be successful or victorious in: as, to ''win'' a game or a battle.
{{smaller block|<poem>Th’ report of his great acts that over Europe ran,
In that most famous Field he with the Emperor ''wan''.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, iv. 314.}}
<poem>He that would ''win'' the race must guide his horse
Obedient to the customs of the course.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', Truth, l. 13.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
'''4.''' To accomplish by effort; achieve, effect, or execute; succeed in making or doing.
{{smaller block|He coulde neuer in one hole daye with a meately good wynde ''wynne'' one myle of the course of the water.
{{right|''Peter Martyr'' (tr. in Eden’s First Books on America, [ed. Arber, p. 163).}}
<poem>Thickening their ranks, and wedged in firm array,
The close-compacted Britons ''win'' their way.</poem>
{{right|''Addison'', The Campaign.}}}}
'''5.''' To reach; attain to; arrive at, as a goal or destination; gain; get to.
{{smaller block|Ye wynde inforced so moche and so streyght ayenst vs that our gouernoures sawe it was not possyble for vs to ''wynne'' nor passe Capo Maleo.
{{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 63.}}
Before they could ''win'' the lodge by twenty paces, they were overtaken.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, ii.}}
<poem>Soon they ''won''
The top of all the topful heav’ns.</poem>
{{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, v. 761.}}
<poem>And when the stony path began
By which the naked peak they ''wan'',
Up flew the snowy ptarmigan.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, iii. 1.}}}}
'''6†.''' To cause to attain to or arrive at; hence, to bring; convey.
{{smaller block|<poem>Toax in the toile out of tene broght,
''Wan'' hym wightly away wondit full sore.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6980.}}
<poem>He sall fordo thi fader syn,
And vnto welth ogayne him ''win''.</poem>
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 70.}}
<poem>Do that I my ship to haven ''winne''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Anelida and Arcite, l. 20.}}
“Sir,” quod she, “I knowe well youre will is not for to haue me I-loste.” “I-loste,” seide he, “nay, but I-''wonne'' to grete honour.”
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 671.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
'''7.''' To gain the affection, regard, esteem, compliance, favor, etc., of; move to sympathy, agreement, or consent; gain the good will of; gain over or attract, as to one’s self, one’s side, or one’s cause; in general, to attract.
{{smaller block|Thy virtue ''wan'' me; with virtue preserve me.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney.''}}
<poem>She ’s beautiful, and therefore to be woo’d;
She is a woman, therefore to be ''won.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 3. 79.}}
<poem>His face was of that doubtful kind
That ''wins'' the eye, but not the mind.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Rokeby, v. 16.}}}}
'''8.''' To prevail on; induce.
{{smaller block|Cannot your Grace ''win'' her to fancy him?
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iii. 1. 67.}}
<poem>Who eas’ly being ''won'' along with them to go,
They altogether put into the wat’ry plain.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, i. 430.}}}}
'''9.''' In ''mining'', to sink down to (a bed of coal) by means of a shaft; prepare (a bed of coal) for working by doing the necessary preliminary dead-work: also applied to beds of ironstone and other ores. [Eng.] {{smaller|In the United States the word ''win'', as used in mining, has frequently a more general meaning; it is thus defined in the glossary of the Pennsylvania Survey: “To mine, to develop, to prepare for mining.” See ''winning''.}}
{{smaller block|The shaft [at Monkwearmouth] was commenced in May, 1826; it was continued for eight and a half years before the first workable coal was reached; and it was only in April, 1846, twenty years afterwards, that the enterprise was proved successful by the ''winning'' of the “Hutton Seam.” {{right|''Jevons'', The Coal Question (2d ed.), p. 68.}}}}
